import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Bootstrap Tooling Strategy — Rust Tooling on the Generic Substrate","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Bootstrap Tooling Strategy — Rust Tooling on the Generic Substrate","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z","status":"implemented","status_note":"Implemented. Amends [ADR-0014](../0014-formatter.md),\\n[ADR-0015](../0015-linter.md), and\\n[ADR-0029](../0029-cli-as-capability-orchestrator.md): their generic,\\nplugin-driven designs are **retained in Rust**; what changes is that the\\ncanonical tooling is now concrete, arrow-surface-focused, and shipped by\\nimproving the existing plugin crates. Depends on\\n[ADR-0074 — Arrow Surface Syntax v0.1](./0074-arrow-syntax-v0_1-surface-redesign.md)\\nand [ADR-0052 — Arrow Calculus Kernel](./0052-arrow-calculus-kernel.md).\\nAmended by decision 2026-09: the Rust substrate (plugin system,\\n`plugin-ext-*`, `plugin-engine-*`) is **kept and improved, not deleted** —\\nthe move to handl is a future port, not a rewrite.\\n\\nImplemented (merged to main)."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0085-bootstrap-tooling-strategy.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0085-bootstrap-tooling-strategy.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0085-bootstrap-tooling-strategy.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0085-bootstrap-tooling-strategy-—-rust-tooling-on-the-generic-substrate" tabindex="-1">ADR-0085: Bootstrap Tooling Strategy — Rust Tooling on the Generic Substrate <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0085-bootstrap-tooling-strategy-—-rust-tooling-on-the-generic-substrate" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0085: Bootstrap Tooling Strategy — Rust Tooling on the Generic Substrate”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The self-hosting goal is for the CLI, formatter, linter, typechecker, test runner, and eventually the LSP server to be written in handl itself. That port can only happen once handl is stable — and to <em>get</em> stable, the stdlib and real programs must be written in handl first, which requires production-grade tooling today.</p><p>The good news from an inventory of the existing platform: a substantial, already-generic Rust substrate exists and works — the plugin system (<code>platform</code> crate: <code>Capability</code>/<code>Plugin</code>/<code>Kit</code>/<code>Host</code>), the capability extensions (<code>plugin-ext-*</code>), and the generic engines (<code>plugin-engine-lex</code>, <code>plugin-engine-parse</code>, <code>plugin-engine-parse-pratt</code>, <code>plugin-engine-exec*</code>, <code>plugin-engine-typecheck*</code>). Much of the work is done; it just was never focused into a concrete toolchain for the arrow surface.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Build <strong>the canonical tooling by improving the existing generic <code>plugin-*</code> crates</strong> — they already <em>are</em> the tooling; no parallel <code>tooling-*</code> crates are created:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Crate(s) to improve</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Formatter</td><td><code>plugin-ext-fmt</code> (canonical style hardcoded in its rules)</td></tr><tr><td>Linter</td><td><code>plugin-ext-lint</code> (canonical rule set over the generic registry)</td></tr><tr><td>Typechecker</td><td><code>plugin-engine-typecheck</code>, <code>plugin-typecheck-core</code>, <code>plugin-typecheck-nbe</code></td></tr><tr><td>Test runner</td><td><code>plugin-lib-test</code> (plus its capability wiring)</td></tr><tr><td>CLI</td><td><code>host-cli</code> + <code>plugin-ext-cli</code> (thin dispatch over the above)</td></tr><tr><td>Shared diagnostics</td><td><strong>new <code>plugin-ext-diag</code></strong> (see ADR-0091) — one home for <code>Diagnostic</code>/<code>Span</code>/<code>Fix</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Rules of engagement:</p><ol><li><strong>The substrate stays and is the implementation.</strong> The plugin system, <code>plugin-ext-*</code>, and <code>plugin-engine-*</code> crates are not replaced — they are worked on, improved, and extended in place. Tool features land in the existing plugin crates through the same <code>Capability</code> contracts.</li><li><strong>Best practices in Rust, now.</strong> Composability, extensibility, modularity, testability, and replaceability are requirements — the same properties the future handl tools will have. Improving in place is not a license for coupled monoliths.</li><li><strong>Canonical style/rules are hardcoded; the machinery is generic.</strong> What may be coupled to the v0.1 surface (ADR-0074) is the <em>rule set</em> and <em>canonical preferences</em>. The engines, pipelines, and extension points underneath stay generic (per-language capabilities per ADR-0041), so other handl languages still benefit.</li><li><strong>Minimal decency, not prototyping quality.</strong> Small module boundaries inside each plugin crate, <strong>100% test coverage</strong> enforced in CI, and one shared diagnostics type family in <code>plugin-ext-diag</code> (ADR-0091) so fmt/lint/typecheck/test/CLI speak the same diagnostics language.</li><li><strong>The CLI stays thin.</strong> <code>host-cli</code> gains/keeps subcommands that dispatch through the existing capability contracts; no <em>new</em> plugin protocol is invented. The full generic capability-registry CLI of ADR-0029 remains the design, exercised for real by this toolchain.</li><li><strong>Port to handl in the future, improve in place until then.</strong> When handl is stable, each tool moves to handl per its RFC (<code>project/rfcs/</code>) — a port of already-generic code, not a rewrite. The Rust substrate remains the bootstrap and fallback implementation afterwards.</li></ol><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li>We can finally write the best handl code in handl itself: stdlib, tests, and examples get fmt/lint/typecheck/test from day one.</li><li>No mass deletion and no duplicate crates: the existing <code>plugin-*</code> tooling crates keep their tests, their modularity, and their users, and are improved in place. Diagnostics are consolidated in <code>plugin-ext-diag</code> (ADR-0091) instead of accumulating per-crate variants.</li><li>Style and rule changes are Rust edits until the handl port — acceptable while only the v0.1 surface exists, and cheap because rules are isolated modules.</li><li>The future handl port is lower-risk: the RFCs port <em>generic, tested</em> Rust designs whose contracts are already exercised.</li></ul>`,11)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};